{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgHzhMrmy5XqaA1JF3Mxj","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The test is unaffected tomorrow. It never opens `tracker.json`. It writes its own five-issue snapshot to a temporary file and passes that path to `weekly_report()`. The hourly rewrite can't change the result.\n\nThe cost is that the test checks `weekly_report()`'s counting logic, not the live file's contents. If a sync produced a malformed export, such as a missing `state` key, this test would still pass.\n\nIn this copy, `sync.sh` only prints \"no tracker configured\" and doesn't touch the file. In the real deployment it does rewrite the file.\n\nIf you want the test to read the live file, there are two ways:\n1. **Recount in the test.** The test would count `\"state\": \"open\"` entries itself and compare that to `weekly_report()`. It stays stable as the file changes. It is weaker, because it repeats the code's own logic.\n2. **Pin the file in the repo.** Keep a fixed copy of the export, such as `tests/fixtures/tracker.json`, and test against that. This is the same idea as my snapshot, but the data is a real file you can inspect.\n\nI kept the inline snapshot as the simplest option. I can switch to either of these if you prefer."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":368,"cache_read_input_tokens":40154,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":368},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"61979c33-651a-46bb-890a-a008e3073df2","uuid":"fb1f3e81-9101-4e00-b8fa-ac85c7b2fa12","timestamp":"2026-10-22T21:36:06.858Z","request_id":"req_011CgHzhMce8LWW2Hz19cwx6"}
